-- title:
--   Endomorphism with β²-sβ+2=0 is Vélu's 2-isogeny
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field and $W$ a Weierstrass curve over $k$ which is elliptic, and let $\beta$ be an endomorphism of the additive group of affine points $W(k)$ belonging to [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalHomSet k W W`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L28), that is: either $\beta = 0$, or there are bivariate polynomials $n_X, d_X, n_Y, d_Y$ over $k$ and a finite set $B \subseteq k$ such that for every nonsingular affine point $(x,y)$ with $x \notin B$ the values of $d_X$ and $d_Y$ at $(x,y)$ are nonzero and $\beta(x,y) = (n_X/d_X, n_Y/d_Y)$ evaluated there. Assume, for an integer $s$, the relation $\beta \circ \beta + 2\,\mathrm{id} = s\beta$ in $\operatorname{End} W(k)$, that $x^2 - sx + 2 \neq 0$ for every integer $x$, and that $2 \neq 0$ in $k$. Let $(x_0,y_0)$ be a nonsingular affine point of $W$ with $W.\mathtt{veluGy}(x_0,y_0) = -(2y_0 + a_1x_0 + a_3) = 0$, so that the point has order two, with $\beta(x_0,y_0) = 0$, and assume the discriminant of the Vélu quotient curve $W.\mathtt{veluQuotient2}\,x_0\,y_0$ — the Weierstrass curve with the same $a_1, a_2, a_3$, with $a_4' = a_4 - 5G$ and $a_6' = a_6 - b_2 G - 7x_0G$, where $G = 3x_0^2 + 2a_2x_0 + a_4 - a_1y_0$ — is nonzero. The conclusion is that there is a Weierstrass variable change $\gamma_0$ over $k$ with $\gamma_0 \bullet W = W.\mathtt{veluQuotient2}\,x_0\,y_0$ such that, for every point $P$ of $W(k)$, $\beta(P)$ is the image of $\mathtt{veluPointMap2}(P)$ under the bijection [`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.equivOfVariableChangeEq`](def/WeierstrassCurve_VariableChangePointEquiv.html#L152) from the points of the quotient curve to the points of $W$ induced by $\gamma_0$; here $\mathtt{veluPointMap2}$ sends the point at infinity and every affine point with $x$-coordinate $x_0$ to the point at infinity, and any other affine point to the point of the quotient curve given by Vélu's order-two formulae.
--
--   This is the degree-two case of the first step of Deuring's argument, identifying the quotient of $W$ by the kernel of an endomorphism whose norm equals the order of that kernel with $W$ itself: the hypotheses force $\ker\beta$ to be the group of order two generated by $(x_0,y_0)$, so $\beta$ factors through Vélu's $2$-isogeny, and the resulting isogeny of the quotient onto $W$ is an isomorphism, hence a change of Weierstrass coordinates. It is used by [`ModularCurve.placeWidthChar_eq_one_of_restrictAlong_ne`](thm.html#ModularCurve.placeWidthChar_eq_one_of_restrictAlong_ne).
-- source:
